docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:26:- **main (MPM + global Poisson):** plausible bed deformation, but (a) the pressure solve consumes ~70% of a ~30 ms frame and sparse RBGS (PR #18, 1.93×) is its practical ceiling — Jacobi-CG, adaptive iterations, and indirect dispatch all failed because the real bug is operator inconsistency (compact stride-1 solve vs central stride-2 divergence, A ≠ D·G → a divergence floor no solver beats); (b) water pools above the bed unreasonably long because Darcy resistance is formulated as a divergence penalty in the pressure RHS, which imposes a pressure-jump entry barrier at the bed surface — inconsistent with the correct Stokes–Darcy interface conditions (flux + stress continuity, not pressure continuity); (c) the bed is a fixed scaffold with a scalar porosity — no granular mechanics at all.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:50:- R8. Dispatches/frame and max-storage-buffers-per-entry-point are first-class budgets, recorded from rung L0 onward via `Solver::profile()`. Each rung also pre-registers a maximum dispatch count and a substep ceiling (with a rough bandwidth estimate) before its implementation starts, so the L3 wall-clock gate cannot be discovered unreachable only after the L1/L2 investment. Sparsity uses over-dispatch + early-out, never `dispatch_workgroups_indirect`.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:51:- R9. Scaling is ~linear in particle count to 200k (ramped: measured at each rung; 200k is the L3 target, not an L0 gate), and real-time is a gated absolute number, not just structure: ≤33 ms/frame median at 200k particles on the reference device (Apple M5) is the L3 exit gate — a perfectly linear 90 ms/frame solver fails the program. Each earlier rung pre-registers a wall-clock ceiling for its representative scene when its gate tests are written; exceeding a ceiling is a halt condition alongside the physics gates. WGSL compiles unmodified for native + web; WebGPU baseline limits except the storage-buffer request.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:61:- KTD-1 — **Two velocity fields per grid node (water + solid), APIC particle↔grid transfers.** Percolation requires co-located phases at *different* velocities (relative Darcy flux); contact requires momentum exchange on a shared grid. One field per phase satisfies both; velocity-averaging couplers (Genesis) are structurally rejected. APIC (per-particle affine matrix C) is the momentum carrier that makes cavities — its absence is precisely why XPBD craters failed. Per the v1 data-path lesson (`docs/issues/010-separate-water-bed-data-paths.md`), water and solid particles occupy separate buffer ranges with phase-specific dispatches, not a shared branchy buffer.
