docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:129:- **Approach:** Mirror the xpbd module shape: one Pod `Params` byte-mirrored in WGSL with a size assert; auto-layout pipelines via `make`/`bg` closures; stable global binding indices; all passes through a `dispatch_pass`-style helper feeding `Profile`. Separate water/solid particle ranges in the canonical `ParticleBuffers` layout so `ui::Renderer` works unchanged. Resolve the 9-vs-16 storage-buffer discrepancy here: pack grid fields into vec4 lanes first, raise `NEEDED_STORAGE_BUFFERS` only if required, and verify a web build still gets the device.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:130:- **Patterns to follow:** `src/solvers/xpbd/mod.rs` (Params, pipelines, dispatch_pass, freeze/apply comments); `src/engine/registry.rs` three-edit pattern and its in-module catalog test (auto-extends via `SolverId::all()`); `tests/phase0.rs` runtime solver-switch test.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:131:- **Test scenarios:** registry row exists for the new `SolverId` (extend the existing registry test's coverage); `build` + one `step` on an empty scene leaves finite state and returns cached metrics without stalling; `Profile.dispatches_per_frame` > 0 and recorded; `particles()` returns the canonical layout with a populated `phase_tag` buffer for a water-only scene (the existing `tests/render_smoke.rs` already validates rendering for any conforming `ParticleBuffers` — no twofield mirror needed).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:132:- **Verification:** `cargo test` green including existing suites untouched; a minimal `examples/twofield_phase0.rs` (build + one step on an empty scene, assert finite state and `dispatches_per_frame > 0`) runs clean — the existing `phase0_noop` example only exercises the noop solvers and proves nothing here; budget numbers (dispatches, max buffers/entry point) printed in the test log.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:152:- **Approach:** MAC-consistent divergence/gradient/Laplacian family on the fine grid; coarse grid (ratio an implementation choice, start 1/4³) with operators built as consistent restrictions (Galerkin or rediscretized-with-verification); coarse solve (few cheap sweeps on ≤ ~11k cells), prolongate as pressure seed, then a fixed budget of fine Jacobi sweeps and projection of the porosity-weighted divergence (φ_f = 1 everywhere in this unit). Solid-cell Neumann pressure conditions at walls are part of the MAC-consistent operator family — boundary stencils are inside the consistency requirement, not bolted on. Optional adaptive-Tait predictor behind an opt-in gate defaulting off. Sparse execution via over-dispatch + early-out on empty tiles.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:155:- **Test scenarios:** operator unit gates run BEFORE any fluid test (the production MMS/code-verification pattern, Salari-Knupp): discrete adjointness `u·(Gp) = −(Du)·p` to machine precision for random fields on the staggered layout, the same check for the coarse restriction/prolongation pair, a manufactured-solution order-of-accuracy sweep on D/G/L (observed log-log error slope matches the formal order), and — decisively — the same checks on the ASSEMBLED projection operator `A = −D·M⁻¹·G` exactly as used in the solve (mass/φ weighting, solid masks, boundary rows included), at both fine and coarse-with-transfer levels, on masked domains representative of real scenes (closed tank, sloped wall, V60 cone, partially filled bed) — a self-consistent D/G pair must not be able to pass while the seeded solve injects pressure through a different operator, and boundary-adjacent restriction/prolongation is exactly where multigrid-like schemes break, so box-only tests are insufficient; a two-grid convergence factor measured on known low- and high-frequency modes of those masked domains; then post-projection RMS divergence — measured with the projection's own divergence operator, interior cells only (surface cells are gated in U4) — drops by at least a stated factor (≥2×) per doubling of the fine-sweep budget until it falls below a pre-registered absolute tolerance, where the tolerance is derived from the visible-error budget (the xpbd density-band envelope) and the sweep count needed to reach it must fit the rung's pre-registered wall-clock ceiling (meeting tolerance only at non-real-time sweep counts fails L0 now, not L3 later); because the self-measured divergence gate is gameable, independent volume gates run alongside it: particle-density drift band, tank water-height drift, zero net flux through closed walls, free-surface fill-fraction conservation; a floor that plateaus above tolerance FAILS the gate (monotonic-but-plateauing was exactly main's A≠D·G signature); run on both a static tank and mid-splash dam break; A/B coarse-seed-on vs off shows the seed removes low-frequency residual (tank pressure profile approaches hydrostatic); with the adaptive-Tait predictor enabled, an A/B run must show a measurable fine-iteration-budget reduction at equal divergence tolerance — below threshold the predictor is removed rather than shipped dormant; density band on settled dam break matches the existing xpbd water-gate envelope; cost gate — dispatches/frame within the recorded U1 budget + a stated increment; long-run (≥2000 steps) settled tank stays settled (no pressure-driven creep or popcorn).
